{"commit": "498de3fa707a4889395850e88e8260261258bbd2", "id": "498de3fa707a4889395850e88e8260261258bbd2-1", "language": "rust", "reason": "Selected test is not present at the fixed revision: tests::test_request_with_invalid_token_delimiter", "repo": "https://github.com/seanmonstar/httparse", "reproduction": {"attempt": "20260924T001805Z-17c5b21f", "evidence_sha256": {"evidence/498de3fa707a4889395850e88e8260261258bbd2-1/20260924T001805Z-17c5b21f/origin.json": "55f9e72cbf89374be9d5317e34ce274acf61aca5a46befc3ceab40f00c57c29f", "evidence/498de3fa707a4889395850e88e8260261258bbd2-1/20260924T001805Z-17c5b21f/proposal.json": "1ee1f4295c4fc2c2c10917c49ee3d0b3ffbedbbb865ec75427b394885bc417b1"}, "provenance": "verbatim"}, "shard": "httparse", "status": "blocked", "subject": "stop parsing requests with invalid method or path delimiters", "summary": "`parse_token` terminated on SP, CR, or LF, so `\"GET\\r / HTTP/1.1\"` parsed successfully with method `\"GET\"` even though the method/path boundary must be a single SP. The fix restricts the terminator to SP, causing any other non-token byte to error.", "updated_at": "2026-09-24T00:18:05.897361+00:00"}
{"commit": "c0631f26e86157a8110a202c112b0ea7a051025b", "id": "c0631f26e86157a8110a202c112b0ea7a051025b-1", "language": "rust", "reason": "Selected test is not present at the fixed revision: tests::test_response_no_cr", "repo": "https://github.com/seanmonstar/httparse", "reproduction": {"attempt": "20260924T001822Z-9e72a80a", "evidence_sha256": {"evidence/c0631f26e86157a8110a202c112b0ea7a051025b-1/20260924T001822Z-9e72a80a/origin.json": "b37fc49474b019888abeee3e0f629f7aaf9abde883ecc6fdb7375f16d3f976ff", "evidence/c0631f26e86157a8110a202c112b0ea7a051025b-1/20260924T001822Z-9e72a80a/proposal.json": "d8714d582e3b5b529a377281ebaab5c4add7bf01d83c20854d3a42e714c5fd1c"}, "provenance": "verbatim"}, "shard": "httparse", "status": "blocked", "subject": "Fix parsing responses that have no reason phrase followed by no CRs (#96)", "summary": "When the status line ended in bare LF with no reason phrase, `Response::parse` set `reason = Some(\"\")` without advancing the byte-slice cursor, so the next header-name slice started inside the status-line digits (producing names like `\"200\\nContent-type\"`). The fix calls `bytes.slice()` to snap the cursor before parsing headers.", "updated_at": "2026-09-24T00:18:22.521007+00:00"}
